DA-004 — Scene 05 — Source Hunt — Approved Draft v1.1

Eli had to grab the camera before Martin reached the end of the corridor.

He came out of the Rider Room with the strap twisted around his wrist and the room key still in his other hand. Martin was already crouched beside the wall opposite their door, looking at the baseboard where it met the carpet.

“Hang on.”

Martin touched one finger to his lips without looking back.

A television was playing two rooms down. Someone laughed on the soundtrack, then an audience did. The elevator machinery gave a low mechanical hum behind the bank of doors. Rain ticked faintly at the window at the far end of the hall.

Martin pressed his fingertips to the painted wall, then gave it one light rap.

Eli raised the camera.

Martin rapped again several feet farther down.

The second sound was flatter. It died where he made it.

“What are you checking?” Eli asked.

“Walls.”

“I can see that.”

Martin stood and walked to the next guest-room door. He did not touch it. He looked at the number, then back toward their room as if the distance between them mattered.

“Bathroom wall should be somewhere along here,” he said.

“You think that’s the next room?”

“Could be. I can’t tell from here.”

Eli almost smiled. “That’s why we’re looking.”

Martin looked at the camera.

Eli kept filming.

They moved toward the elevators. Martin stopped at each break in the corridor—the recessed ice alcove, the return vent near the ceiling, the short cross-hall leading toward the stairs. He listened first and touched things second. A vent grille buzzed faintly when the air system came on. He put two fingers against it until the vibration stopped, then waited for the fan cycle to begin again.

“Okay,” Eli said quietly toward the camera. “We’re checking whether anything in the hall can carry—”
“Just film the vent.”
Eli lowered the camera a few inches. “Why?”
“Because it’s vibrating.” Martin pointed upward. “Get that. Elevator hum if it starts again. Doors. Pipes.”
“I’m trying to explain why we’re out here.”
Martin looked at him. “Then say I heard knocks. Don’t tell them what I think about them.”
Eli kept the camera up for another second, then stopped recording.

The red icon vanished. For a few seconds the only moving light was the floor indicator over the elevators descending from five to four.

The car stopped on their floor.

Both men waited.

The doors parted on an empty cab.

A muted thump traveled through the wall behind the elevator bank as the mechanism settled. It had some of the broad quality of the rumble from the room, though not the hard edge of the knocks.

Martin put his palm against the wall.

“There,” Eli said. “That could be the rumble.”

“Could be.”

“You heard that in the room?”

“Something like it.”

“That’s good.”

Martin gave him a quick look.

“I mean for the test.”

The doors closed again. The low vibration passed under Eli’s shoes and disappeared.

Martin walked to the stairwell door and opened it. The concrete enclosure beyond was brighter than the hall, with painted block walls and a metal handrail running down to the next landing. The acoustics changed immediately. The latch clicking shut behind them produced a sharp report that seemed much larger than the movement that caused it.

Martin reopened the door halfway and let the closer pull it in.

The latch struck.

Eli flinched despite expecting it.

“Again,” Martin said.

He repeated it. Same hard strike. Same short bloom of sound up the stairwell.

“Not the same,” Eli said.

“No.”

“But close enough that from inside the room—”

“Not from here.”

“You keep saying that like it’s bad news.”

Martin stepped back into the corridor.

Eli followed.

At the bend near the service boundary they had passed earlier, the public carpet stopped at a narrow painted door with a STAFF ONLY plaque. Martin tried the handle once. Locked.

He did not try again.

He looked along the wall instead, then back the way they had come. “The employee passage we were in earlier ran behind the public side for part of the tour.”

“Different floor.”

“I know.”

“So this doesn’t tell us anything.”

“It tells us this building has spaces we can’t see from the guest side.”

Eli raised the camera again. “Which means a sound could move through them.”

“Or a person could be in them.”

“That too.”

Martin bent near the service door and listened at the gap. Nothing distinct came through. He straightened and looked up at the ceiling where a pipe chase disappeared behind a boxed soffit.

“You really want this to be pipes,” Eli said.
Martin kept his attention on the wall. “I want a source.”
Eli’s grip tightened on the camera.
“Earlier tonight you would’ve gone back to the room by now.”
Martin straightened and started walking.
Eli followed. “Now you’re checking every door on the floor.”
Martin did not answer.

Martin stopped at a housekeeping alcove recessed from the corridor. The cart was gone, but a pair of folded towels sat on a shelf beside a plastic bin. A narrow plumbing access panel was set low in the wall. Martin crouched beside it and listened.

Eli aimed the camera at him.

“You’re taking it seriously,” Eli said.

Martin looked up. “We already did this.”

“Not this part.”

“What part?”

“The part where you’re the one who won’t quit.”

Martin stood.

Eli expected a denial. Instead his father looked past him toward the Rider Room.

A knock sounded somewhere down the corridor.

Both men turned.

Several seconds passed.

A second knock came, farther away or softer.

Then, after a gap long enough to break any rhythm Eli could hold in his head, a guest-room door opened near the elevators. A man stepped out carrying an empty ice bucket. He pulled the door closed behind him.

The latch made another hard knock.

Martin exhaled.

It was not dramatic. His shoulders dropped perhaps half an inch. His jaw loosened.

Eli noticed anyway.

“There,” Martin said.

“What?”

“That travels.”

“The door?”

“The first sound could have been a door. Second too.”

“Not the ones in our room.”

Martin did not answer.

“But you relaxed.”

Martin looked at him.

“You did.”

The man with the ice bucket walked past them, nodded once, and continued toward the machine. Eli waited until he had gone around the bend.
“What exactly did you hear in there?”
Martin’s mouth tightened.
“The knocks.”
“You got up before I even knew what I was listening to.”
Martin glanced toward the camera.
Eli lowered it but did not turn it off.
“What was different?”
“Not now.”
Eli waited, but Martin had already started toward the elevators.
Eli followed him. “If it was just a knock, you wouldn’t be doing this.”

Martin pressed the elevator button.

“We’re on the same floor as the room,” Eli said.

“I know.”

“So where are we going?”

“Nowhere.”

The elevator arrived. Martin did not enter. He listened to the doors open, to the small motor above them, to the soft impact when they reached their stops. Then he stepped back and let them close.

Eli stared at him.

Martin said, “I wanted to hear it from here.”

“And?”
“Not close enough.”
The elevator doors closed between them and the empty cab.

For the next ten minutes they worked their way back toward the room. Martin tested single surfaces without repeating the cadence: wallboard, the wooden trim beside the stairwell, the metal push plate on the public door, the base of the vent grille. Eli recorded each sound. Some carried farther than either expected. One pipe gave a hollow tick after a nearby faucet shut off. A door two rooms away produced a sharp impact that seemed to come from the wall beside them. The elevator produced a low vibration close enough to the earlier rumble that Eli logged it as a plausible analogue.

None of it gave them the sequence from the room.

Eli tried once to prompt the corridor.

“Can you make the same sound again?”

Martin turned on him so quickly that Eli stopped before adding anything else.

“What?”

“We’re checking sources.”

“I know.”

“Then check sources.”

“You’re scared it’ll happen again.”

Martin looked down the corridor.

Eli lowered the camera a fraction. An hour ago he would have taken Martin’s silence as victory. He had wanted exactly this: his father unable to dismiss something, unable to turn it into a loose hinge or a draft or a story somebody had told before they arrived.
Now Martin looked less convinced than cornered.

Eli lifted the camera again. “Just tell me what changed.”

“Not now.”

They returned to the Rider Room without another knock.

The chairs were still by the window with the round table between them. The bathroom door was open. The fan remained off. The recorder on the dresser was still running, its level bars rising and falling with the small sounds they brought back into the room.

Martin checked the display and then the time.

“We need to charge your camera,” he said.

“That’s it?”

“For now.”

“We didn’t find the source.”

“No.”

“We found ways sound can travel.”

“Yes.”

“But nothing that made that pattern.”

Martin looked at him.

Eli waited.

Martin took off his shoes and sat on the edge of the bed.

Eli plugged the camera charger into the wall beside the dresser. The charging light came on.

He should have felt closer to what he had wanted. Martin had heard the knocks. Martin had searched harder than Eli had and come back without a source.
Yet the ordinary door latch in the corridor had made Martin’s shoulders drop.
The one-then-two in the Rider Room had sent him straight to the walls.

